regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:2:r"""#549 fbctrlportkill -- the DETECTOR for dev_fbctrl.c's port selector, on an UNMODIFIED `-E testmips`.
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:3:
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:4:machine_test.c installs `fbctrl addr=0x12f00000` in testmips's own setup (and in default_test() for the other test
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:5:machines).  Every access below is a real guest sb/sh/sw/sd/lw placed with `put w` and executed through real address decode
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:6:and memory_rw -- stepped one instruction at a time, except session F, which free-runs to a breakpoint.  No machine or
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:7:device is added to reach the site.  t0 = 0xffffffffb2f00000, kseg1 of DEV_FBCTRL_ADDRESS; PORT is +0x00, DATA +0x10.
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:8:
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:9:THE DEFECT (HEAD before #549): the PORT arm STORED any selector with only a warning, and the DATA arm then called exit(1)
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:10:on the stored int -- `sw 9` to PORT followed by any DATA access ended the emulator process.  #549 REFUSES a selector >= 8
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:11:at the PORT arm (the previous, valid selector is kept) and announces the refusal once per device, `(once)`.
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:12:
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:13:SESSIONS (each on a fresh emulator)
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:14:  C  controls: PORT=5, DATA=0x2a, read both back (LIVENESS); X1=123, COMMAND=GET_RESOLUTION, read X1 (DEVICE SIGNATURE:
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:15:     the model zeroes X1/Y1 without X11 -- RAM at +0x10 would read back 2, the last value written there, and a banked
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:16:     register WITHOUT command execution would read 123); no `(once)` line may appear (nothing is refused).
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:17:  A  accept: all EIGHT selectors, written at widths 1, 2, 4 and 8, each read back through PORT; ports 1-7 round-trip
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:18:     a distinct DATA value (port 0 is COMMAND and is written only with NOP).
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:19:  P  refusal, anchored to a previous selector: PORT=5 (DATA 0x2a), then nine out-of-range writes -- 9, 8, 16 (sw),
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:20:     0x200000000, 0x100000003, all-ones (sd), all-ones (sw), 0xff (sb), 0x100 (sh) -- each PRECEDED by a fresh
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:21:     PORT=5 and followed by a PORT read that must still say 5, so every row fails only on its own value; then
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:22:     PORT=7 and a refused 9 must leave 7.  The `(once)` count must be exactly 1 right
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:23:     after the FIRST refusal and still 1 after the last.
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:24:  K  the witness pair, repaired: PORT=5, DATA=0x2a, PORT=9 refused, then a DATA READ (must survive and read port 5's
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:25:     0x2a) and a DATA WRITE of 0x77 (must land in port 5: read back through PORT=5).
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:26:  F  free-running: one refused PORT write, then `continue` to a breakpoint.  Stepping raises debug()'s verbosity, so a
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:27:     refusal announced with debug() instead of fatal() would pass every stepped row; free-running it does not print.
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:28:
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:29:CONTRACT (clause e).  CATCHES: the store reached for an out-of-range value (or a clamped/masked/reset one), the compare
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:30:narrowed or signed (bit 63, bits 32-33, 32-bit truncation), a boundary slip at 8, a refusal applied to some widths only,
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:31:a legal selector refused, a refusal that rewrites the previous selector, a latch never set / set late / keyed per value,
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:32:an announce demoted to debug(), and the pre-#549 process exit on DATA (kill table 1: 20 of 20 such edits red,
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:33:regress/artifacts/fbctrlportkill_20260924/table1/TABLE.out).  NOT CLAIMED: a process-global latch instead of the
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:34:per-instance field (every machine installs ONE fbctrl; adding a device to reach a second would be laundering); the
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:35:deletion of the DATA arm's range check, which a correct PORT arm makes unreachable -- it is KEPT as a tripwire, and it
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:36:matters beyond style: `warned_port` sits right after `port[8]`, so an index of 8 that got past a broken PORT arm would
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:37:land on the latch itself; and the device's OTHER two process-ending calls -- fbctrl_command()'s default (any command
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:38:other than 0-2, including the documented but unimplemented FILL/COPY; a refused selector at power-on leaves COMMAND
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:39:selected, so a following DATA write of such a value reaches it) and the access function's default (any offset other
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:40:than 0x00/0x10).  Those are filed.  Session P re-selects PORT=5 before each refusal: kill table 1 ran without the
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:41:re-select, and one corrupted selector then turned every later "keeps 5" row red, overstating which row catches
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:42:what (the flagship seat, pass 1).
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:43:
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:44:Every stepped word is compared with the emulator's own step line (the hand-assembled-encoding trap).
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:45:Output: one row per check, then FBCTRLP_CONTROL=OK|FAILED, FBCTRLP_RESULT=n/t, FBCTRLP_DONE.
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:46:usage: fbctrl_port_probe.py <gxemul-binary>
regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py:47:"""
